`Cache.set` now takes an optional `ttlSeconds` and sends `SET key value EX ttlSeconds` when it's given. Without it, the command is still `SET key value`. The change is in `src/cache.js` and is uncommitted.

```js
async set(key, value, ttlSeconds) {
  if (ttlSeconds === undefined) return this.send(`SET ${key} ${value}`);
  if (!Number.isInteger(ttlSeconds)) throw new TypeError('ttlSeconds must be an integer');
  return this.send(`SET ${key} ${value} EX ${ttlSeconds}`);
}
```

I added one thing you didn't ask for: a non-integer TTL throws a `TypeError` before anything is sent. That keeps a string like `"60 foo"` from being written into the line protocol. If you'd rather pass values through and let the server reject them, it's a one-line delete.

The existing test suite can't pass in this environment. It needs a cache server on port 6399, and the repo doesn't include one. There's also no local `redis-server`. The test fails the same way before and after my change (`connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:6399`). So I checked the wire format with a throwaway fake server on port 6400 instead, and deleted that script afterward.

Checked:
- `npm test` → `# fail 1` with `error: 'connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:6399'` (exit 1). This is the pre-existing environment failure, not a regression.
- Fake-server run (`node` on a temp script, since deleted) → `wire: ["SET k1 v1","SET k2 v2 EX 60"]`. `set('k3','v3','60')` threw `TypeError ttlSeconds must be an integer`.

Not checked: the real cache server, so I haven't confirmed the server accepts `EX` in this form. The line protocol is assumed to be Redis-style from the `SET`/`GET` usage. Also, `ttlSeconds: 0` is passed through as `EX 0`; I didn't decide how the server handles that.

`Cache.set` now takes an optional `ttlSeconds`. When it's given, the client sends `SET key value EX ttlSeconds`; when it's omitted, the line is still `SET key value`.

The change is in `src/cache.js`:

```js
async set(key, value, ttlSeconds) {
  const ttl = ttlSeconds === undefined ? '' : ` EX ${ttlSeconds}`;
  return this.send(`SET ${key} ${value}${ttl}`);
}
```

I also added two tests to `test/cache.test.js`. They start a small local TCP stub that records the request line, then check that `set` without a TTL sends `SET k v` and `set` with 60 sends `SET k v EX 60`. Both pass.

The existing `set then get` test still fails, but that's unrelated to this change. It needs a real cache server on port 6399, and none is running in this environment (`ECONNREFUSED`). I haven't run it against a live server, so the end-to-end path is only verified against the stub.

I didn't validate `ttlSeconds` beyond the undefined check. Passing `0`, a negative number, or a non-number will be sent as-is, and the server will decide what to do with it. Tell me if you want that checked on the client side.
